#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120660} :activity/id #uuid "cecba37f-b75d-52b8-abe7-f9ee46fa930b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049188 :topic/id #uuid "e21ada02-529b-5965-affd-a2fc02d1a1e8" :topic/math-academy-id 1781} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120660 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121030 :tutorial/content "In general, the LU factorization $A=LU$ of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ is the product of two matrices $L$ and $U,$ where\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1 &  & 0 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ⋯ & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis a *unit* lower triangular matrix, and\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ & ⋯ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗ &  & ∗ \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis an upper triangular matrix.\n\nAs it turns out, there is a quick way of finding a matrix's LU factorization, which is particularly helpful when factoring large matrices. \n\nLet's see a concrete example of how this works." :tutorial/id #uuid "c9e96109-bac0-51c2-bc4d-1ea84862f08d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3180 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5322eb81-b15a-5d70-90c5-5379814950f5" :step/math-academy-id 17365 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120858}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120661 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119797 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119608 :question/id #uuid "5eca7964-4613-5fd6-b952-778886687830" :question/math-academy-id "e-4771"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a86c24c2-97c2-534e-9c03-9735b031bb23" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302371}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 4x4 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "6cf3492e-895b-5506-8979-4faa419a170a" :step/math-academy-id 17366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120662 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119798 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119609 :question/id #uuid "0ec2663e-be00-560b-8d42-d0e1b1d023c2" :question/math-academy-id "e-4772"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d216cc0e-58a7-5fab-b5fe-60c7656338cc" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243992}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Missing Numbers in the Matrix L of an LU Factorization"} :step/id #uuid "89e38a16-8fd1-5c11-a05c-c0db788b3f97" :step/math-academy-id 17367 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120799}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120798 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119884 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119704 :question/id #uuid "763fad5f-7cb7-5ff3-a14e-e2849e90906a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8924"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c159d750-ab1f-538b-b8cc-ae66ef1ae414" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051083}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399594}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Missing Numbers in the Matrix U of an LU Factorization"} :step/id #uuid "74265631-3af2-5e6b-b11d-7e6f8574a338" :step/math-academy-id 33312 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120662}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120799 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119885 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119705 :question/id #uuid "b19414c3-443f-5075-a2db-f27eb3a7e936" :question/math-academy-id "e-8925"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e28bd3d9-649d-5cc3-a12e-e1f821caaf24" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241135} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302335}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 3x3 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "cf3d4e48-300b-5ab5-8d9f-2a92b1ce3217" :step/math-academy-id 33317 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120661}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120858 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121114 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the LU factorization $A=LU$ of the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe proceed by reducing $A$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination.\n\nTo start, we want to make $0$ at the position $a_{{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}1}}.$ To do this, we take the ${\\color{blue}2}$nd row of $A,$ and we add the ${\\color{red}1}$st row of $A$ multiplied by ${\\color{purple}-3}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We take the factor $\\color{purple}-3$ that we multiplied $R_{\\color{red}1}$ by, change its sign, and write it as the entry $a_{{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}1}}$ with a box around it. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ \\,3\\, & -2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\,\\,(∗)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important**: \n\n- You can think of the box $\\bbox[FloralWhite, 2pt, border-radius: 5px; border: 1px solid black]{\\color{purple}\\:\\phantom{3}\\:}$ as representing the original zero.\n\n- In addition, we store the value $\\color{purple}3$ to use later.\n\n- When doing this in practice, you might find it helpful to write a large zero with a small $\\color{purple}3$ inside it. This is represented by $\\bbox[FloralWhite, 2pt, border-radius: 5px; border: 1px solid black]{\\color{purple}\\:3\\:}$ in our matrix above.\n\nLet's now continue with our Gaussian elimination.\n\nWe want to make $0$ at the position $a_{{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}1}}.$ To do this, we take the ${\\color{blue}3}$rd row of $(\\ast)$ and we add the ${\\color{red}1}$st row multiplied by ${\\color{purple}-2}.$ Then, we place $-({\\color{purple}-2})={\\color{purple}2}$ with a box around it at the position $a_{{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}1}}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ \\,3\\, & -2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ \\,3\\, & -2 & -2 \\\\ \\,2\\, & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we want to make $0$ at the position $a_{{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}2}}.$ To do this, we take the ${\\color{blue}3}$rd row and we add the ${\\color{red}2}$nd row multiplied by ${\\color{purple}2}.$ Then, we place $-({\\color{purple}2})={\\color{purple}-2}$ with a box around it at the position $a_{{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}2}}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ \\,3\\, & -2 & -2 \\\\ \\,2\\, & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ \\,3\\, & -2 & -2 \\\\ \\,2\\, & -2 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix is now in row echelon form (remember that the boxes each represent zero), and we're almost done.\n\nNow, here's the neat bit. Both $L$ and $U$ can be extracted from the matrix above:\n\n- The matrix $L$ takes all elements that lie *below* the main diagonal:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRemember that $L$ must be *unit* lower triangular.\n\n- The matrix $U$ takes the remaining elements:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRemember that $U$ must be upper triangular." :tutorial/id #uuid "f1dfa3d1-aa1b-5761-b962-fe95e204e499" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6123 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "1a8c2638-bb86-56f5-8124-4aab0df917e4" :step/math-academy-id 37192 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120798}}] :activity/title "LU Factorization of NxN Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120003}]}
